Hmm. Gunyouki seems an even more rare version of the word than gunyoki. 

Google says:
Results 1 - 9 of about 10 for gunyouki -nethack -jnethack -samurai.
Results 1 - 17 of about 25 for gunyoki -nethack -jnethack -samurai.

And verifying the resulting pages show that most of them seem to refer 
to military aircraft rather than Nethack.

When searching for gunyoki, I also found this picture:
http://xent.com/~bsittler/treo600/images/000324d7_Picture852_17Apr04.jpg

My theory:
Gunyoki is a (slang?) term for the kind of hot fast food stuff seen in 
the picture, the name derived from it meaning a 'bomber plane' or 
'fighter plane'. Somehow the Nethack devteam picked up this, but the 
writer of the blog linked in the OP has not, it might be local slang 
etc, hence his/her amusement at this use of the word.

Do I have to make examples of similar items? It is too early in the day 
to quaff a B-52.

